00:00:00.560 --> 00:00:02.710 align:start position:0%
 
attention is the most precious resource  that we can leverage to actually get  things done increase productivity and  achieve highest performance in whatever  we do  focus is not something we have it's a  skill we gain by training our brain  our brain is finely attuned to  distraction so today's digital  environment makes it especially hard to  focus  thanks to a variety of sources of  entertainment news socialization and  more work it is easy to get distracted  not only do distractions slow us down  they can cause serious errors  to prevent this we should develop better  mental focus  research has shown that individuals who  can sustain their attention for long  periods of time performed better on all  sorts of cognitive challenges than those  who cannot  i guess we can all agree that no matter  what goals profession or work you are  currently pursuing a disciplined mind is  critical for your success  so how can we train our brain to stay  focused  the brain is the most complex biological  structure in the human body  around 100 billion neurons are  communicating via 1 million billion  connections that are sending signals up  to 1 000 times each second which is one  quadrillion events every second of your  waking life  the brain can be divided into sections  with different roles from breathing and  heart rate to coordinating movement and  involuntary reflexes  the most developed parts the neocortex  hold memories our abilities to plan  think and imagine hope and  dream the brain's building blocks are  not exactly simple either  neurons are not just wires they alter  and process information  synapses where signals are handed over  form one neuron to the next contain  receptors for hundreds of chemical  signals opening them up to outside  influence  given how complex our brain is can we  actually train it to become more focused  there is plenty of research showing that  it is possible  first stop multitasking  multitaskers might seem superhuman but  they pay a big price according to a  stanford study  in a sample of 100 stanford students  about half identified themselves as  media multitaskers the other half did  not  the test examined students attention  spans memory capacity and ability to  switch from one task to the next and the  multi-taskers performed more poorly on  each test  multitasking reduces our cognitive  abilities due to the fact that the brain  can only fully focus on one task at a  time  to stop multitasking make a list each  day of what you need to accomplish and  then choose one task at a time  if the other things on your list come to  mind while you are working on a chosen  task just remind yourself that they are  already on your list so they will get  taken care of  if you think of new things that you need  to do just jot them down on your list  and then go back to your current task  give your full attention to the task at  hand to improve the quality and speed of  work  second treat your mind like a muscle  everyone knows that when you lift  weights your muscles get bigger and you  get stronger  however when you stop exercising muscles  shrink and you get weaker  new research shows that the brain is  more like a muscle it changes and gets  stronger when you use it  training your brain to stay focused is a  matter of practice  every time you experience distracting  thoughts you have to acknowledge them  for what they are time and attention  killers  whenever you feel like you must stop  working you should push it a little  further  if you have big trouble focusing right  now you shouldn't try to work for 60  minutes straight  build your levels of focus gradually  first two weeks try focusing for 20  minutes take a five minute break then go  again  in two months you should be able to stay  focused for 60 minutes and get a lot of  things done  third find your peak hours  by studying thousands of people  researchers found that we are truly  focused for an average of only six hours  per week  this means that you want to be really  diligent with what you put into those  hours  some people perform better during the  day while others do great during the  night  to find your most productive hours or  the peak hours you should actively  measure your productivity during various  hours of the day  pay attention to your energy thought  patterns distractions motivation and  moods while experimenting different work  hours  then allocate your toughest tasks for  those moments  make sure you allow at least a week to  establish these peak hours  so if you try to get your most important  tasks done from 8 am to 10 am experiment  it for a week  next week focus your attention on  critical tasks starting from 10 am and  ending at 12 pm  over time this will help you identify  your best performing hours  fourth  avoid distractions you can't train your  brain to stay focused unless you ensure  that your external environment also  lacks distractions  while it may sound obvious people often  underestimate just how many distractions  prevent them from concentrating on the  task at hand  your phone for example should never  disturb your attention  turn off the notifications say no  whenever someone interrupts you and be  committed to cut off every possible  distraction around you  minimizing the sources of distraction  isn't always as easy as it sounds  while it might be relatively simple to  turn off your mobile television or radio  you might find it much more challenging  to deal with an interrupting co-worker  spouse child or roommate  one way to deal with this is to set  aside a specific time and place and  request to be left alone for that period  of time  another alternative is to seek out a con  location where you know you will be able  to work undisturbed  fifth maintain realistic to-do lists  in an effort to complete as much as  possible every day we are tempted to  fill our daily to-do lists with a wide  variety of tasks and no breaks in  between  while we may be able to perform most of  the activities in such a list the ones  occurring in the latter part are bound  to suffer  this is because we are already fatigued  by the time that we get to them  as such be realistic about your to-do  lists and include only the kind of  activities which you were sure that you  can do with the resources at hand  moreover include breaks in between these  tasks  they give you a chance to relax at  specific times of your day  this is known as the pom doro technique  of time management  according to management experts this  technique allows you to rejuvenate  yourself and refresh your brain in  between tasks  as a result your focus is laser sharp  once you resume your work  as such be realistic about your working  capacity and take breaks throughout your  day  sixth take a short break  have you ever tried to focus on the same  thing for a long period of time  after a while your focus starts to break  down and it becomes more and more  difficult to devote your mental  resources to the task  not only that but your performance  ultimately suffers as a result  traditional explanations in psychology  have suggested that this is due to  attentional resources being depleted but  some researchers believe that it has  more to do with the brain's tendency to  ignore sources of constant stimulation  researchers have found that even taking  very brief breaks by shifting your  attention elsewhere can dramatically  improve your focus  so the next time you are working on a  prolonged task be sure to give yourself  an occasional mental break  shift your attention to something  unrelated to the task at hand even if it  is only for a few moments  these short moments of respite might  mean that you are able to keep your  mental focus sharp and your performance  high when you really need it  seventh  create daily habits and routines  developing new habits and routines might  also help improve your ability to focus  a routine works to train your brain for  the activities that will follow  it conditions your brain to expect the  work that you have in store  by following a routine you are able to  perform the same activity right before  you begin working  as such this activity in your routine  acts as a warm-up for your brain  it can be as simple as making a cup of  coffee or as vigorous as a session of  exercise  intentionally repeat the good behavior  at least 30 times  researchers believe that humans need to  have a repetitive event at least 30  times before it becomes a new normal  our brains recognize routines and  proceed to create the neural pathways  that are necessary to complete the tasks  signified by the routines  as such to boost your ability to focus  create new habits and routines  your brain will adapt and condition  itself for the tasks within your day  hate  practice mindfulness  practicing mindfulness is another way to  help increase one's focus  in one study researchers analyzed a  group of hr professionals  the managers had to engage in  simulations of the sort of complex  multitasking they engaged in each day at  work  these tasks had to be completed in 20  minutes and included answering phones  scheduling meetings and writing memos  with information pouring in from  multiple sources including by phone  calls emails and text messages  some of the participants received eight  weeks of training in the use of  mindfulness meditation and the results  found that only those who had received  this training showed improvement in  concentration and focus  members of the meditation group were  able to stay on task longer switched  between tasks less frequently and  performed the work more efficiently than  the other groups of participants  practicing mindfulness can involve  learning how to meditate but it can also  be as simple as trying a quick and easy  deep breathing exercise  ninth improve sleep  numerous studies have shown that proper  sleep helps improve concentration and  focus  when we are well rested we can pay  better attention to new information that  we come across in our daily experiences  besides sleep helps make new information  stick  during sleep the brain replays memories  from the day making the neural  connections stronger  this helps us remember the things we  experienced when we were awake  different stages of sleep play a role in  forming different types of memory such  as learning how to do something like  playing a piano compared to learning  facts  sleep also helps our creativity helping  us find new solutions to problems by  looking at things in a new way while we  sleep you may have heard people say they  will sleep on it in order to solve a  problem or make a decision  therefore it is important to try and get  as close to the recommended amount of  sleep as possible on most nights  for most adults it is seven to eight  hours of sleep per night  tenth  make time for exercise  physical exercises like running swimming  and weightlifting aren't just good for  the body they promote brain health too  numerous studies have shown that  physical exercise is closely related to  the cognitive function through a cascade  of cellular and molecular processes that  promote angiogenesis neurogenesis and  synaptogenesis thus enhancing learning  memory and brain plasticity  scientists think regular exercise may  help stimulate the release of a chemical  called brain derived neurotrophic factor  which some research suggests helps  rewire memory circuits to improve their  functioning  therefore if you want to train your  brain to stay focused and productive  make sure to exercise on a regular basis  if you found this video useful i would  appreciate it if you smash the like  button  also make sure to subscribe to the  coding tech channel and click on the  notification bell  enjoy the rest of your day

